(2320003, 315954, 15242, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by petrakka:</b></div><hr/><i> that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot.<br/> <br/>Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The lens could be the Peleng as well.<br/> <br/>Great photo, congrats on the ribbon!</td>', 'Originally posted by petrakka: that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot. Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man.   The lens could be the Peleng as well. Great photo, congrats on the ribbon!', '2006-04-17 19:12:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320034, 315954, 11832, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LoudDog:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by petrakka:</b></div><hr/><i> that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot.<br/> <br/>Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The lens could be the Peleng as well.<br/> <br/>Great photo, congrats on the ribbon! </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Peleng is so much wider. Unless he did some serious cropping, Id say its safe to assume its a peleng</td>', 'Originally posted by LoudDog: Originally posted by petrakka: that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot. Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man.   The lens could be the Peleng as well. Great photo, congrats on the ribbon!  Peleng is so much wider. Unless he did some serious cropping, Id say its safe to assume its a peleng', '2006-04-17 19:22:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320041, 317329, 32254, '<td valign="top">This was one of my favs in this challenge, can''t believe it didn''t score higher, by far the best b-ball shot IMO. You have an outstanding portfolio all around as well.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-04-17 18:27:12.</i></td>', 'This was one of my favs in this challenge, can''t believe it didn''t score higher, by far the best b-ball shot IMO. You have an outstanding portfolio all around as well.', '2006-04-17 19:26:55.000000', '2006-04-17 19:27:12.000000', 0),
(2320138, 318992, 42198, '<td valign="top">Great capture, and in mid-air as well. Nice timing!<br/> <br/>What lets it down for me is the on-camera flash, which creates the very harsh shadows in the background (around the dog, and behind the stick he''s jumping) - Try shooting late afternoon with no flash, the lighting will be in your favour.</td>', 'Great capture, and in mid-air as well. Nice timing! What lets it down for me is the on-camera flash, which creates the very harsh shadows in the background (around the dog, and behind the stick he''s jumping) - Try shooting late afternoon with no flash, the lighting will be in your favour.', '2006-04-17 20:04:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320170, 318794, 40718, '<td valign="top">OK, a few things that catch my eye.<br/> <br/>1) The composition is good.  Vertical crop accentuates the jump.  Rule of thirds is used well.<br/> <br/>2) The color of the water is bad.  Not that you could do much, but that shade brown for water is a turn off.<br/> <br/>3) If you could have done it over, I would have suggested an even higher shutter speed to absolutely freeze the water splash.<br/> <br/>4) All that being said, I appreciated the serendipity of capturing this shot.  It does deserve some credit for that, but voters don''t know how easy or hard a shot is and thus tend to not give you a bonus for it.</td>', 'OK, a few things that catch my eye. 1) The composition is good.  Vertical crop accentuates the jump.  Rule of thirds is used well. 2) The color of the water is bad.  Not that you could do much, but that shade brown for water is a turn off. 3) If you could have done it over, I would have suggested an even higher shutter speed to absolutely freeze the water splash. 4) All that being said, I appreciated the serendipity of capturing this shot.  It does deserve some credit for that, but voters don''t know how easy or hard a shot is and thus tend to not give you a bonus for it.', '2006-04-17 20:16:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320173, 318992, 43148, '<td valign="top">Sorry, I posted this in a forum, but not here, which was a bit silly!! LOL We took these in the late dusk time on Sunday night, just thought of it then, do NOT ask me why!! Anyway, took about fifty shots and used the best two we had, one for me and one for jorr!! Knew going in we should have reshot them, but kinda late by then and I needed an entry, so for these entries, we DO understand what we did incorrectly!<br/> <br/>Jacque</td>', 'Sorry, I posted this in a forum, but not here, which was a bit silly!! LOL We took these in the late dusk time on Sunday night, just thought of it then, do NOT ask me why!! Anyway, took about fifty shots and used the best two we had, one for me and one for jorr!! Knew going in we should have reshot them, but kinda late by then and I needed an entry, so for these entries, we DO understand what we did incorrectly! Jacque', '2006-04-17 20:17:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320178, 318563, 40718, '<td valign="top">OK, you''ve probably forgotten more about photography than I know, but here goes...<br/> <br/>1) I would have gone with a single light source.  The multiple shadows tend to confuse the eye.<br/> <br/>2) With that, perhaps upping the contrast.  I know I''m leading to a slightly different shot overall, but I''m trying to add a bit more dynamics.<br/> <br/>3) That warm gray looks more like a mauve on my monitor.  Not a fan.<br/> <br/>4) If you are happy with it, then who the hell cares? ;)</td>', 'OK, you''ve probably forgotten more about photography than I know, but here goes... 1) I would have gone with a single light source.  The multiple shadows tend to confuse the eye. 2) With that, perhaps upping the contrast.  I know I''m leading to a slightly different shot overall, but I''m trying to add a bit more dynamics. 3) That warm gray looks more like a mauve on my monitor.  Not a fan. 4) If you are happy with it, then who the hell cares? ;)', '2006-04-17 20:20:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320185, 318323, 40718, '<td valign="top">Heh, you must have missed the abolutely raging thread about two months back about using the radial blur.<br/> <br/>Overall the shot lacks both a subject and enough contrast.  I do think it does play tricks with my eyes and that''s good, but I want a "subject", even if the real subject is the ground.  The square just isn''t enough to keep us captivated.</td>', 'Heh, you must have missed the abolutely raging thread about two months back about using the radial blur. Overall the shot lacks both a subject and enough contrast.  I do think it does play tricks with my eyes and that''s good, but I want a "subject", even if the real subject is the ground.  The square just isn''t enough to keep us captivated.', '2006-04-17 20:22:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320250, 318928, 30861, '<td valign="top">I like this picture quite a lot, but there are many elements working against it in this challenge. Primary among these is that it is not obvious that the deer is actually leaping the fence; it takes close examination to deduce that a/he''s not standing on the ground beyond the fence. In other words, the shot''s not dynamic enough to shout LEAP at the viewer.<br/> <br/>Then, there are three very distracting elements in the image: the  trash on the right side, the structure in the back, and the disembodied deer rump on the left. All of these are what I call "eyetraps", pulling our attention away from where it should be. Finally, the rightmost deer''s face is hidden by a post of some sort, and this is mildly disturbing as well.<br/> <br/>Finally, the dreamy PP use of glow, while attractive in itself, is serving to effectively isolate and empahsize two of the distracting elements; the disembodied rump and the piece of trash.<br/> <br/>So, all in all, the image is fairly static (the leap is implied rather than expressed) and is compositionally chaotic (the eye bounces around like a pinball) and this, IMO, is why a potentially fine image failed to score higher.<br/></td>', 'I like this picture quite a lot, but there are many elements working against it in this challenge. Primary among these is that it is not obvious that the deer is actually leaping the fence; it takes close examination to deduce that a/he''s not standing on the ground beyond the fence. In other words, the shot''s not dynamic enough to shout LEAP at the viewer. Then, there are three very distracting elements in the image: the  trash on the right side, the structure in the back, and the disembodied deer rump on the left. All of these are what I call "eyetraps", pulling our attention away from where it should be. Finally, the rightmost deer''s face is hidden by a post of some sort, and this is mildly disturbing as well. Finally, the dreamy PP use of glow, while attractive in itself, is serving to effectively isolate and empahsize two of the distracting elements; the disembodied rump and the piece of trash. So, all in all, the image is fairly static (the leap is implied rather than expressed) and is compositionally chaotic (the eye bounces around like a pinball) and this, IMO, is why a potentially fine image failed to score higher.', '2006-04-17 20:44:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320257, 318111, 55480, '<td valign="top">I love this one.</td>', 'I love this one.', '2006-04-17 20:48:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320285, 315954, 38800, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by hsteg:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LoudDog:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by petrakka:</b></div><hr/><i> that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot.<br/> <br/>Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The lens could be the Peleng as well.<br/> <br/>Great photo, congrats on the ribbon! </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Peleng is so much wider. Unless he did some serious cropping, Id say its safe to assume its a peleng </i></td></tr></table> <br/>No cropping was done to the photo, zenitar sounds about right. I''ll find out for sure and post it.</td>', 'Originally posted by hsteg: Originally posted by LoudDog: Originally posted by petrakka: that russian lens was probably the zenitar which has good and bad copies out, but mostly its not too hot, but who cares about pixel peeping when u got the shot. Only thing that would make this better is a powerful fill flash and an underexposed sky, but its still pretty solid, congrats man.   The lens could be the Peleng as well. Great photo, congrats on the ribbon!  Peleng is so much wider. Unless he did some serious cropping, Id say its safe to assume its a peleng  No cropping was done to the photo, zenitar sounds about right. I''ll find out for sure and post it.', '2006-04-17 21:08:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(2320341, 315954, 25952, '<td valign="top">if there was no cropping this was a zenitar, i can tell you right now =)</td>', 'if there was no cropping this was a zenitar, i can tell you right now =)', '2006-04-17 21:40:40.000000', NULL, 0),
